\chapter{Tutorial: Analysing ProgSnap2}
\label{tut:analysing-progsnap2}

The previous tutorial ended with ProgSnap2 export; this one turns that
dataset into a report.
The learner first generates a complete report, then narrows it with
[[Column=Regex]] boundaries so the effect of the filter syntax becomes
visible against a stable dataset.

\ltnote{%
  This chapter uses contrast between whole-dataset analysis and
  range-restricted analysis.
  The critical aspect is that [[learnlog analyse]] filters by event
  ranges, not isolated matching events, which is why the boundary syntax
  is introduced only after the learner has seen the unfiltered report.
}

<<[[analysing-progsnap2.md]]>>=
---
id: analysing-progsnap2
title: Analyse a ProgSnap2 dataset
summary: Generate LaTeX reports and narrow them with Column=Regex ranges.
---

# Start from a ProgSnap2 export

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - learnlog export --progsnap2
hint: Reuse an earlier export or create a fresh `.progsnap2` file.
```

If you do not already have a ProgSnap2 zip, create one from a learnlog
project with `learnlog export --progsnap2`.

# Generate a LaTeX analysis report

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - learnlog analyse
  - -f
  - -o
hint: A typical command is `learnlog analyse -f course.progsnap2 -o report.tex`.
```

Generate a report from the dataset and save it to a `.tex` file.
The report groups edits and runs into the edit--run cycles that the
analysis command reconstructs.

# Produce a standalone document

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - --standalone
hint: Add `--standalone` when you want a complete LaTeX document.
```

Repeat the analysis with `--standalone` if you want a complete document
that can be compiled directly, rather than a fragment for inclusion.

# Filter from a tagged start point

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - X-Tag=lab1
hint: Quote the boundary so the shell keeps it as one argument.
```

Pass a start boundary so the report keeps only the events from the
first matching tag onward, for example:

`learnlog analyse 'X-Tag=lab1' -f course.progsnap2 -o report.tex`

# Filter a tagged range

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - X-Tag=lab1
  - X-Tag=lab2
hint: Pass two `Column=Regex` boundaries to restrict the analysis window.
```

Add an end boundary as well, for example:

`learnlog analyse 'X-Tag=lab1' 'X-Tag=lab2' -f course.progsnap2 -o range.tex`

This keeps the analysis focused on the sessions between the two tagged
regions.
